  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several strategies for modifying borohydride-based materials. Overall, it shows that these compounds can be tuned by mixing them with other hydrides or salts, replacing individual ions or molecular groups, confining them inside porous hosts, or adding foreign elements as dopants or catalysts.\n\nAt the top left, “composites” illustrates reversible combinations of a borohydride with another metal hydride, forming mixed systems rather than a single new crystal. Nearby, “combination” shows a more integrated mixing of different ionic components, suggesting the formation of mixed-cation or mixed-anion materials. The substitutions panel highlights chemical replacement at the molecular level: an amine-like unit or a cation/hydrogen-related species is exchanged, changing the local bonding around boron and nitrogen.\n\nThe lower middle “nanoscaffold” panel depicts borohydride species confined within nanoscale pores or channels, implying that a host framework can stabilize or alter the behavior of the hydride. Finally, the “dopants and catalysts” panel shows small added particles dispersed in a gray material, indicating catalytic or kinetic enhancement without fully changing the bulk structure.\n\nTaken together, the figure conveys a toolkit for tailoring hydrogen-storage materials through composition, substitution, confinement, and additives.",
